#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""fpms-telemetry-ros - camera and NPU HEALTH from MQTT onto the ROS graph.

Usage:
    fpms-telemetry-ros          run in the foreground (systemd Type=simple)

WHY THIS NODE EXISTS
====================
The operator dashboard reads the rover through rosbridge, and rosbridge only
delivers what is inside `topics_glob` in /etc/fpms/rosbridge_params.yaml. Two of
the four subsystems it shows had NO ROS PUBLISHER ANYWHERE:

  * the camera - fpms_rover_agent.py encodes frames and sends them straight to
    MQTT `telemetry/camera`; nothing mirrors any of it onto the graph;
  * the NPU    - fpms-npud publishes `telemetry/npu` and raises its faults on
    `events/npu_fault`, also MQTT only.

So the dashboard renders both tiles as NO ROS SOURCE - hatched grey, deliberately
neither good nor bad (assets/app.js, linkTiles()). That choice was right: the
alternative is guessing green, and a green tile over a blind fire-detection rover
is the single worst thing this system can produce. But grey is not an answer, it
is an admission, and this node is the answer.

It is the same shape as fpms_lidar_ros.py - subscribe to the agent's MQTT,
republish onto ROS - and the same shape as fpms_foxglove_cmd.py's mission
mirror, whose /fpms/mission/* topics this dashboard already consumes.

WHAT IT DOES NOT DO: FRAMES
===========================
It does not carry the picture. `telemetry/camera` embeds a base64 JPEG of about
40 kB every ~170 ms; pushing that through rosbridge is a different engineering
problem (a 10 MB max_message_size, a websocket shared with the LiDAR, and a
browser decoding base64 at 6 Hz) and the dashboard does not ask for it - it asks
whether the camera is ALIVE. So the encoder output is read for its shape and
then dropped on the floor: see _camera_fields(), which whitelists the keys it
keeps and never retains `frame`. That is structural, not a convention - the
40 kB string cannot reach a publisher because it is never stored.

THE STALE CONTRACT - the sharp edge, and it is sharper here than on the LiDAR
============================================================================
ARRIVAL DOES NOT IMPLY FRESHNESS. Two of the three sources publish on a TIMER:

  telemetry/pose   every ~5 s from fpms_rover_agent.heartbeat(), ALWAYS, whether
                   or not the camera has produced a single frame. `camera_ok`
                   inside it is a flag the agent last wrote, not an observation
                   made now.
  telemetry/npu    every FPMS_NPU_TELEMETRY_S from fpms-npud's watchdog thread,
                   unconditionally AND INCLUDING WHILE FAULTED - deliberately,
                   so "the NPU is broken" is distinguishable from "fpms-npud is
                   not running".

Only telemetry/camera is data-driven: the agent publishes it inside the `if ok:`
after cv2.imencode() of a frame it just read, so one message means one frame
actually existed. That distinction is load-bearing and is the reason a fresh
frame is allowed to clear a latched camera fault below, while a fresh heartbeat
is never allowed to clear anything.

Every value published here is therefore aged from THE MOMENT THIS PROCESS
RECEIVED IT, on time.monotonic(), never from a timestamp inside the payload -
the same rule assets/feeds.js states as non-negotiable, for the same two
reasons: a stuck publisher re-stamps a dead sensor, and the Pi's clock and the
laptop's clock do not agree and are not synchronised at a venue with no NTP.

THE UNKNOWN CONTRACT - do not invent a value when the source is silent
======================================================================
If telemetry/npu has never arrived, this node publishes "unknown". Not 0, not
"ok", not false. NPU_SPEC.md design rule 3 in the daemon's own words: never
claim a detection you cannot substantiate - and "nothing is wrong" is a claim.

Mechanically there are three classes of topic and the class decides the rule:

  ALWAYS      /fpms/camera/state, /fpms/camera/health, /fpms/npu/state,
              /fpms/npu/health. Published at FPMS_TELEMETRY_ROS_HZ from process
              start, with zero MQTT arrivals, carrying the literal string
              "unknown". These four are what a tile keys off, and they are the
              only topics that can say "I am running and my source is silent" -
              a statement no absent topic can make.

  WHILE FRESH every scalar. /fpms/npu/p50_ms is published while telemetry/npu is
              fresh and STOPS when it is not. It is never zero-filled and never
              frozen. Absence is a failure mode ROS already handles correctly;
              a confident stale number is not - fpms_lidar_ros.py stops
              publishing LaserScans for exactly this reason.

  ONCE SEEN   /fpms/camera/stale and /fpms/camera/frame_age_s. Omitted until a
              first frame has arrived (before that there is no age to report,
              and `stale=false` would be a lie about a camera nobody has heard
              from), then published forever, including while stale - they are
              the topics ABOUT staleness and going quiet would defeat them.

THIS NODE CANNOT MOVE THE ROVER
===============================
Every topic it publishes begins "/fpms/" and is a READ-OUT. There is no
/cmd_vel, no /cmd_duty, no /estop, and no MQTT publish path AT ALL - the paho
client here is subscribe-only and this file contains no call to client.publish().
_assert_publishers() enforces the topic rule at startup and refuses to start if
it is ever broken, because a comment saying "this node cannot move the rover"
decays and a check that aborts the process does not. Same guard, same reasoning
as fpms_foxglove_cmd._assert_no_actuators().

TOPICS PUBLISHED - all inside `topics_glob`, which is NOT widened
================================================================
/etc/fpms/rosbridge_params.yaml whitelists "[/estop,/fpms/*,...]" with fnmatch
semantics where '*' matches '/' too, so /fpms/* already covers every name below
and NOTHING IN THE WHITELIST NEEDS TO CHANGE. That is why the "/fpms/" prefix is
asserted rather than assumed: a topic outside it would be accepted by ROS,
published happily, and silently never reach the browser.

    /fpms/camera/state                std_msgs/String   unknown|ok|warn|stale|off|fault
    /fpms/camera/health               std_msgs/String   JSON snapshot
    /fpms/camera/stale                std_msgs/Bool
    /fpms/camera/frame_age_s          std_msgs/Float32
    /fpms/camera/fps                  std_msgs/Float32  ARRIVALS here, measured
    /fpms/camera/resolution           std_msgs/String   e.g. "640x480"

    /fpms/npu/state                   std_msgs/String   unknown|ok|degraded|stale|fault
    /fpms/npu/health                  std_msgs/String   JSON snapshot
    /fpms/npu/detection_available     std_msgs/Bool
    /fpms/npu/model_loaded            std_msgs/Bool
    /fpms/npu/model_sha_state         std_msgs/String   verified|unverified|mismatch
    /fpms/npu/p50_ms                  std_msgs/Float32
    /fpms/npu/p90_ms                  std_msgs/Float32
    /fpms/npu/infer_per_s             std_msgs/Float32
    /fpms/npu/drops                   std_msgs/Int32
    /fpms/npu/consecutive_failures    std_msgs/Int32
    /fpms/npu/core_mask               std_msgs/String   in force, not requested

    /fpms/npu/fault                   std_msgs/String   events/npu_fault + npu_recovered
    /fpms/agent/fault                 std_msgs/String   events/fault + camera_recovered

MQTT SOURCES (subscribe only, QoS 0 for telemetry, QoS 1 for events)
    fpms/<thing>/telemetry/camera     per frame  (data-driven)
    fpms/<thing>/telemetry/pose       ~0.2 Hz    (TIMER)
    fpms/<thing>/telemetry/npu        ~0.2 Hz    (TIMER, including while faulted)
    fpms/<thing>/events/npu_fault     edge       (and fpms-npud's LWT)
    fpms/<thing>/events/npu_recovered edge
    fpms/<thing>/events/fault         edge       (agent: camera|lidar|heartbeat)
    fpms/<thing>/events/camera_recovered edge

VERIFY THE OUTCOME, NOT THE EXIT CODE
=====================================
paho's subscribe() returns (rc, mid) the instant the packet is queued; it says
nothing about whether the broker GRANTED the subscription. /etc/mosquitto/
fpms.acl names fpms/rover1/# explicitly, so a thing-name drift or an ACL edit
produces a REFUSED subscribe that looks exactly like a silent sensor. So the
SUBACK is checked in on_subscribe(), refusals are named in the log and carried
in `mqtt.refused` inside both health snapshots. Likewise the publisher table is
verified against rclpy's own list at startup rather than trusted, and the
publish plan is checked against that table on every tick so a topic with no
publisher is a logged fault instead of a value that quietly goes nowhere.

This module is importable WITHOUT ROS and WITHOUT paho (both imports are
guarded), so Store, the state machines and the publish plan can be unit-tested
off-robot - the same trick fpms_lidar_ros.py, fpms_odom_tf.py and
deadband_sweep.py use.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import json
import math
import os
import signal
import sys
import threading
import time

try:
    import rclpy
    from rclpy.node import Node
    from rclpy.qos import (QoSProfile, ReliabilityPolicy, DurabilityPolicy,
                           HistoryPolicy)
    from std_msgs.msg import Bool, Float32, Int32, String
    _ROS_AVAILABLE = True
except ImportError:  # pragma: no cover - exercised only off-robot
    _ROS_AVAILABLE = False
    Node = object


# ============================================================================
# TOPICS
# ============================================================================
# The prefix is asserted, not assumed. See _assert_publishers().
#
# fnmatch semantics in rosbridge_params.yaml: '*' matches '/' too, so the single
# entry /fpms/* in topics_glob covers every name in this table. The whitelist
# does not move for this node, which is the whole point of putting everything
# under /fpms/ - "widen the whitelist" is not on the table and never should be.
TOPIC_PREFIX = "/fpms/"

# Named so that a future edit adding an actuator has to delete a line that says
# REFUSING TO START rather than merely ignore a comment.
FORBIDDEN_TOPICS = ("/cmd_vel", "/cmd_duty", "/cmd_enable", "/estop",
                    "/reset_encoders", "/beep", "/servo_s1", "/servo_s2",
                    "/fpms/cmd/stop")

T_CAM_STATE = "/fpms/camera/state"
T_CAM_HEALTH = "/fpms/camera/health"
T_CAM_STALE = "/fpms/camera/stale"
T_CAM_AGE = "/fpms/camera/frame_age_s"
T_CAM_FPS = "/fpms/camera/fps"
T_CAM_RES = "/fpms/camera/resolution"

T_NPU_STATE = "/fpms/npu/state"
T_NPU_HEALTH = "/fpms/npu/health"
T_NPU_DET = "/fpms/npu/detection_available"
T_NPU_LOADED = "/fpms/npu/model_loaded"
T_NPU_SHA = "/fpms/npu/model_sha_state"
T_NPU_P50 = "/fpms/npu/p50_ms"
T_NPU_P90 = "/fpms/npu/p90_ms"
T_NPU_RATE = "/fpms/npu/infer_per_s"
T_NPU_DROPS = "/fpms/npu/drops"
T_NPU_CONSEC = "/fpms/npu/consecutive_failures"
T_NPU_CORES = "/fpms/npu/core_mask"

T_NPU_FAULT = "/fpms/npu/fault"
T_AGENT_FAULT = "/fpms/agent/fault"

# kind -> the std_msgs type published on that topic. Kept as strings so the
# whole table, and every publish plan built from it, is testable with no ROS.
TOPIC_KINDS = {
    T_CAM_STATE: "str", T_CAM_HEALTH: "str", T_CAM_STALE: "bool",
    T_CAM_AGE: "f32", T_CAM_FPS: "f32", T_CAM_RES: "str",
    T_NPU_STATE: "str", T_NPU_HEALTH: "str", T_NPU_DET: "bool",
    T_NPU_LOADED: "bool", T_NPU_SHA: "str", T_NPU_P50: "f32",
    T_NPU_P90: "f32", T_NPU_RATE: "f32", T_NPU_DROPS: "i32",
    T_NPU_CONSEC: "i32", T_NPU_CORES: "str",
    T_NPU_FAULT: "str", T_AGENT_FAULT: "str",
}

ROS_TYPE_NAMES = {"str": "std_msgs/String", "bool": "std_msgs/Bool",
                  "f32": "std_msgs/Float32", "i32": "std_msgs/Int32"}


# ============================================================================
# STATES
# ============================================================================
# "unknown" is a first-class value, not a placeholder. It is what this node says
# when it has no evidence, and it is deliberately distinct from every other
# state so that no consumer can accidentally treat it as good OR as bad.
S_UNKNOWN = "unknown"    # no evidence has ever arrived
S_OK = "ok"
S_WARN = "warn"          # arriving, but slower than it should be
S_STALE = "stale"        # arrived once, has now gone quiet
S_OFF = "off"            # camera only: streaming was turned OFF on purpose
S_FAULT = "fault"        # a declared, named fault
S_DEGRADED = "degraded"  # npu only: serving, but not as configured


def log(*a):
    print(time.strftime("%H:%M:%S"), *a, flush=True)


# ============================================================================
# CONFIG
# ============================================================================

def load_config(path="/etc/fpms/config.env"):
    """Read the same file every other FPMS service reads.

    Credentials live there and only there - never in this file, which is pushed
    to a public repo. Environment wins for the keys this node needs, matching
    fpms_lidar_ros.py, so the unit's Environment= block is not silently inert.
    """
    cfg = {}
    try:
        with open(path) as fh:
            for line in fh:
                line = line.strip()
                if not line or line.startswith("#") or "=" not in line:
                    continue
                k, v = line.split("=", 1)
                cfg[k.strip()] = v.strip().strip('"').strip("'")
    except OSError as e:
        # Not fatal, but it IS the reason a service can retry against the wrong
        # broker forever with nothing in the log. Say it once.
        log(f"config read failed ({e}); falling back to the environment")
    cfg.update({k: v for k, v in os.environ.items() if k.startswith("FPMS_")})
    return cfg


def _f(cfg, key, default):
    try:
        return float(str(cfg.get(key, default)).strip())
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return float(default)


class Thresholds:
    """Freshness bounds, DERIVED from the cadences that produce the data.

    Hard-coding these would let /etc/fpms/config.env change a publish rate while
    the judgement of "too slow" stayed behind - the dashboard and the rover
    disagreeing about the same feed, which assets/feeds.js calls out as the
    thing its per-feed SPEC table exists to prevent. So every bound below is
    computed from the knob that actually sets the rate.
    """

    def __init__(self, cfg=None):
        cfg = cfg or {}
        # The agent's camera pump: FPMS_CAMERA_FPS=6 today, and deliberately so
        # (higher settings saturated the WiFi link and starved the LiDAR).
        fps = max(0.1, _f(cfg, "FPMS_CAMERA_FPS", 6.0))
        self.camera_fps_nominal = fps
        # 18 frame periods, floored at 3 s. At 6 fps that is 3.0 s: long enough
        # that ordinary jitter and one dropped MQTT round trip never trip it,
        # short enough that a dead camera is caught inside one dashboard glance.
        self.camera_stale_s = _f(cfg, "FPMS_CAMERA_STALE_S", max(3.0, 18.0 / fps))
        self.camera_warn_s = _f(cfg, "FPMS_CAMERA_WARN_S", self.camera_stale_s / 2.0)
        # The agent heartbeat is every 5 s (heartbeat() sleeps 50 x 0.1 s).
        # 15 s = three beats; one lost beat must not read as a dead agent.
        self.pose_stale_s = _f(cfg, "FPMS_POSE_STALE_S", 15.0)
        # fpms-npud's own cadence knob, times three, for the same reason.
        npu_period = max(0.5, _f(cfg, "FPMS_NPU_TELEMETRY_S", 5.0))
        self.npu_stale_s = _f(cfg, "FPMS_NPU_STALE_S", npu_period * 3.0)
        # How long we wait, while the agent claims to be streaming, before
        # calling a total absence of frames a fault rather than a cold start.
        # A camera open + MJPG negotiation is seconds, not tens of seconds.
        self.no_frame_grace_s = _f(cfg, "FPMS_CAMERA_GRACE_S",
                                   max(10.0, self.camera_stale_s * 3.0))
        self.publish_hz = max(0.2, _f(cfg, "FPMS_TELEMETRY_ROS_HZ", 1.0))
        self.report_s = _f(cfg, "FPMS_TELEMETRY_ROS_REPORT_S", 30.0)


# A mirrored event is a log line, not a payload channel. 4 kB is far above any
# event this stack emits (the agent truncates its own error to 300 chars) and
# far below anything that would matter on the websocket.
MAX_EVENT_CHARS = 4000


# ============================================================================
# PURE HELPERS - no ROS, no MQTT, no clock. Unit-testable off-robot.
# ============================================================================

def _scrub(obj):
    """Replace non-finite floats with None, recursively.

    Python's json.dumps emits bare NaN and Infinity by default, and its
    json.loads ACCEPTS them - so a NaN produced anywhere upstream survives the
    MQTT hop invisibly and would be re-emitted here into a String the browser
    feeds to JSON.parse(), which throws on "NaN". One malformed float would
    therefore take out the whole health tile with a parse error pointing at this
    node rather than at whatever produced the NaN. Scrub instead: an absent
    number reads as unknown, which is exactly what a NaN means anyway.
    """
    if isinstance(obj, float):
        return obj if math.isfinite(obj) else None
    if isinstance(obj, dict):
        return {k: _scrub(v) for k, v in obj.items()}
    if isinstance(obj, (list, tuple)):
        return [_scrub(v) for v in obj]
    return obj


def to_json(obj, limit=None):
    """Serialise for a std_msgs/String. Must never raise into a publish path."""
    try:
        s = json.dumps(_scrub(obj), allow_nan=False, default=str)
    except (TypeError, ValueError) as e:
        s = json.dumps({"error": f"unserialisable payload: {e}"})
    if limit and len(s) > limit:
        s = json.dumps({"truncated": True, "chars": len(s),
                        "head": s[:limit]})
    return s


def _num(v):
    """A finite float, or None. None means UNKNOWN and is never published."""
    if isinstance(v, bool) or v is None:
        return None
    try:
        f = float(v)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return None
    return f if math.isfinite(f) else None


def _int(v):
    if isinstance(v, bool) or v is None:
        return None
    try:
        return int(v)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return None


def _bool(v):
    """Tri-state. A missing key is UNKNOWN, never False.

    This is the single most important coercion in the file. `bool(None)` is
    False, and a False on detection_available means "the NPU is not detecting",
    which is a hard claim. A key that simply was not in the payload must not be
    able to manufacture it - in either direction.
    """
    if v is None:
        return None
    return bool(v)


def _camera_fields(payload):
    """Whitelist what is kept from telemetry/camera. `frame` is NOT on the list.

    The payload carries a ~40 kB base64 JPEG. Nothing here retains it, so it
    cannot reach a publisher, a health snapshot or a log line by accident. This
    function is the only place the camera payload is read.
    """
    if not isinstance(payload, dict):
        return None
    w, h = _int(payload.get("width")), _int(payload.get("height"))
    dets = payload.get("detections")
    return {
        "width": w,
        "height": h,
        "resolution": (f"{w}x{h}" if w and h else None),
        # The agent's own view of whether ITS in-process NPU path came up. Not
        # the same statement as fpms-npud's detection_available and deliberately
        # published under a different name: the agent has its own legacy NPU
        # block, and conflating the two would hide a half-migrated rover.
        "agent_npu": _bool(payload.get("npu")),
        "detections": len(dets) if isinstance(dets, list) else None,
        "fire_like": _bool(payload.get("fire_like")),
        "fire_ratio": _num(payload.get("fire_ratio")),
        "format": payload.get("format") if isinstance(payload.get("format"), str) else None,
    }


def _npu_fields(payload):
    """Whitelist what is kept from telemetry/npu.

    Whitelisted rather than passed through so that a future field added to
    fpms-npud cannot silently change the size or the shape of what crosses the
    websocket, and so this file's contract with the dashboard is readable in one
    place.
    """
    if not isinstance(payload, dict):
        return None
    return {
        "detection_available": _bool(payload.get("detection_available")),
        "required": _bool(payload.get("required")),
        "model_loaded": _bool(payload.get("model_loaded")),
        "model_sha_state": payload.get("model_sha_state"),
        "model_sha_note": payload.get("model_sha_note"),
        "model_path": payload.get("model_path"),
        "variant": payload.get("variant"),
        "core_mask": payload.get("core_mask"),
        "core_mask_requested": payload.get("core_mask_requested"),
        "core_mask_fallback": _bool(payload.get("core_mask_fallback")),
        "p50_ms": _num(payload.get("p50_ms")),
        "p90_ms": _num(payload.get("p90_ms")),
        "max_ms": _num(payload.get("max_ms")),
        "probe_ms": _num(payload.get("probe_ms")),
        "budget_ms": _num(payload.get("budget_ms")),
        "infer_per_s": _num(payload.get("infer_per_s")),
        "inferences": _int(payload.get("inferences")),
        "latency_samples": _int(payload.get("latency_samples")),
        "drops": _int(payload.get("drops")),
        "timeouts": _int(payload.get("timeouts")),
        "errors": _int(payload.get("errors")),
        "slow": _int(payload.get("slow")),
        "consecutive_failures": _int(payload.get("consecutive_failures")),
        "consecutive_slow": _int(payload.get("consecutive_slow")),
        "reinits": _int(payload.get("reinits")),
        "queued": _int(payload.get("queued")),
        "clients": _int(payload.get("clients")),
        "uptime_s": _num(payload.get("uptime_s")),
        "fault": payload.get("fault"),
        "fault_detail": payload.get("fault_detail"),
        "fault_since": _num(payload.get("fault_since")),
    }


class Rate:
    """Arrival rate, weighted so ONE long gap moves the number a lot.

    Same 0.6/0.4 weighting as assets/feeds.js and the rover agent, and the same
    reason: a smooth average keeps reporting a plausible rate long after the
    source dies, and a plausible-but-frozen number has fooled this project
    twice. A gap longer than the stale bound is not averaged in at all - the
    feed died and came back, so the pre-outage history is not evidence about
    now, and the EWMA restarts from the first interval after the gap.
    """

    def __init__(self, gap_reset_s):
        self.hz = 0.0
        self.last = None
        self.gap_reset_s = gap_reset_s

    def note(self, now):
        if self.last is not None:
            dt = now - self.last
            if dt > 1e-4:
                inst = 1.0 / dt
                if dt > self.gap_reset_s or self.hz <= 0.0:
                    self.hz = inst
                else:
                    self.hz = 0.6 * self.hz + 0.4 * inst
        self.last = now


class Store:
    """Everything this node knows, and when it learned it.

    No ROS, no MQTT, no wall clock: every time argument is a caller-supplied
    time.monotonic() value. That makes the whole state machine testable off
    robot at any simulated age, which is the only way to exercise the stale and
    silent paths without waiting for them in real time.

    Monotonic, not time.time(), because an NTP step or a manual clock change on
    a Pi that has just found a route to the internet must not be able to make a
    stale value look fresh - the same reason feeds.js uses performance.now().
    """

    def __init__(self, thresholds=None, started=0.0):
        self.th = thresholds or Thresholds()
        self.started = started

        self.cam = None            # last _camera_fields(), or None
        self.cam_at = None         # monotonic of arrival
        self.cam_n = 0
        self.cam_rate = Rate(self.th.camera_stale_s)

        self.pose = None
        self.pose_at = None
        self.pose_n = 0
        # (monotonic, frames) of the previous heartbeat, for the agent's own
        # encode rate - an independent cross-check on our arrival rate. If the
        # two disagree the frames are being lost between the agent and here,
        # which is a broker/WiFi problem and not a camera problem.
        self._frames_mark = None
        self.agent_fps = None

        self.npu = None
        self.npu_at = None
        self.npu_n = 0

        # Last NPU event, and whether it currently stands. fpms-npud's LWT
        # publishes fault=daemon_offline on an unclean disconnect, so this can
        # be the ONLY evidence that exists when telemetry/npu never arrived.
        self.npu_event = None
        self.npu_event_at = None
        self.npu_fault_active = None      # str name, or None if recovered

        # Latched camera fault from the agent's events/fault. Cleared by
        # events/camera_recovered OR by a frame arriving - see note_camera().
        self.cam_fault = None
        self.cam_fault_at = None
        self.cam_fault_last = None        # never cleared; history for the JSON

        self.bad_payloads = 0
        self.unknown_topics = 0

        # MQTT link state, so a silent source can be told apart from a broker
        # that never connected. Written by the paho callbacks.
        self.mqtt = {"state": "never", "since": None, "refused": [],
                     "subscribed": 0, "expected": 0}

    # -- ingestion ----------------------------------------------------------

    def note_camera(self, payload, now):
        fields = _camera_fields(payload)
        if fields is None:
            self.bad_payloads += 1
            return False
        self.cam = fields
        self.cam_at = now
        self.cam_n += 1
        self.cam_rate.note(now)
        # A frame is POSITIVE EVIDENCE and is allowed to clear a latched camera
        # fault. That is legitimate here and would not be for the heartbeat:
        # telemetry/camera is published inside `if ok:` after imencode() of a
        # frame the agent just read, so it cannot be produced by a dead camera.
        # telemetry/pose can, and therefore clears nothing.
        if self.cam_fault is not None:
            self.cam_fault = None
        return True

    def note_pose(self, payload, now):
        if not isinstance(payload, dict):
            self.bad_payloads += 1
            return False
        self.pose = {
            "camera_ok": _bool(payload.get("camera_ok")),
            "lidar_ok": _bool(payload.get("lidar_ok")),
            "streaming": _bool(payload.get("streaming")),
            "frames": _int(payload.get("frames")),
            "scans": _int(payload.get("scans")),
            "uptime_s": _num(payload.get("uptime_s")),
        }
        self.pose_at = now
        self.pose_n += 1

        frames = self.pose["frames"]
        if frames is None:
            self._frames_mark = None
            self.agent_fps = None
        else:
            prev = self._frames_mark
            self._frames_mark = (now, frames)
            if prev is not None:
                dt, dn = now - prev[0], frames - prev[1]
                if dn < 0:
                    # The counter went backwards: the agent restarted. The rate
                    # across a restart is meaningless, so report nothing rather
                    # than a negative or a wild number.
                    self.agent_fps = None
                elif dt >= 1.0:
                    self.agent_fps = round(dn / dt, 2)
        return True

    def note_npu(self, payload, now):
        fields = _npu_fields(payload)
        if fields is None:
            self.bad_payloads += 1
            return False
        self.npu = fields
        self.npu_at = now
        self.npu_n += 1
        return True

    def note_event(self, suffix, payload, now):
        """An edge from events/*. Returns the dict to mirror, or None."""
        if not isinstance(payload, dict):
            self.bad_payloads += 1
            return None
        out = dict(payload)
        out["_topic"] = suffix
        out["_rx_monotonic"] = round(now, 3)

        if suffix == "events/npu_fault":
            self.npu_event, self.npu_event_at = out, now
            self.npu_fault_active = payload.get("fault") or "unnamed"
        elif suffix == "events/npu_recovered":
            self.npu_event, self.npu_event_at = out, now
            self.npu_fault_active = None
        elif suffix == "events/camera_recovered":
            self.cam_fault = None
        elif suffix == "events/fault":
            if payload.get("component") == "camera":
                self.cam_fault = payload.get("error") or "unnamed camera fault"
                self.cam_fault_at = now
                self.cam_fault_last = {"error": self.cam_fault,
                                       "at_monotonic": round(now, 3)}
        else:
            self.unknown_topics += 1
            return None
        return out

    # -- ages ---------------------------------------------------------------

    @staticmethod
    def _age(at, now):
        return None if at is None else max(0.0, now - at)

    def camera_age(self, now):
        return self._age(self.cam_at, now)

    def pose_age(self, now):
        return self._age(self.pose_at, now)

    def npu_age(self, now):
        return self._age(self.npu_at, now)


# ============================================================================
# STATE MACHINES
# ============================================================================

def camera_state(store, now):
    """One of unknown|ok|warn|stale|off|fault, and WHY.

    Order matters and is defended line by line below. Read it as: the most
    recent, most direct evidence wins.
    """
    th = store.th
    age = store.camera_age(now)
    p_age = store.pose_age(now)
    pose_fresh = p_age is not None and p_age <= th.pose_stale_s
    pose = store.pose if pose_fresh else None
    streaming = pose.get("streaming") if pose else None
    camera_ok = pose.get("camera_ok") if pose else None

    # 1. A FRESH FRAME OUTRANKS EVERYTHING. It is the only direct observation of
    #    the sensor that exists, and it is at most camera_stale_s old, whereas
    #    the heartbeat's camera_ok can be five seconds behind reality.
    if age is not None and age <= th.camera_stale_s:
        if age > th.camera_warn_s:
            return S_WARN, f"frames arriving but {age:.1f}s since the last one"
        return S_OK, "frames arriving"

    # 2. A declared fault. The agent says the device stopped delivering, or
    #    vanished, or was never found. Named, not inferred.
    if store.cam_fault is not None:
        return S_FAULT, f"agent declared a camera fault: {store.cam_fault}"
    if camera_ok is False:
        return S_FAULT, "agent heartbeat reports camera_ok=false"

    # 3. Streaming turned OFF by an operator command is NOT a fault and must
    #    never be coloured like one. The agent stops publishing telemetry/camera
    #    when STREAM_ENABLED is clear, which is indistinguishable from a dead
    #    camera unless this case is handled explicitly.
    if streaming is False:
        return S_OFF, "streaming is switched off; the agent is not sending frames"

    # 4. Frames were arriving and have stopped.
    if age is not None:
        return S_STALE, f"no frame for {age:.1f}s (bound {th.camera_stale_s:.1f}s)"

    # 5. Never a single frame. Only a fault once the grace window has passed AND
    #    the agent is alive and claims to be streaming - before that this node
    #    may simply have started first, which is the normal case at boot.
    if pose_fresh and streaming is not False:
        if (now - store.started) > th.no_frame_grace_s:
            return S_STALE, ("the agent is alive and streaming but NO camera "
                             "frame has ever arrived here")
        return S_UNKNOWN, "waiting for a first frame"

    # 6. No frames, no live heartbeat: we know nothing about the camera, and
    #    saying so is the whole job.
    return S_UNKNOWN, ("no camera frame and no agent heartbeat have arrived; "
                       "this says nothing about the camera")


def npu_state(store, now):
    """One of unknown|ok|degraded|stale|fault, and WHY."""
    th = store.th
    age = store.npu_age(now)

    if age is None:
        # fpms-npud's last will publishes fault=daemon_offline on an unclean
        # disconnect and may be the only thing we ever hear from it.
        if store.npu_fault_active:
            return S_FAULT, (f"no telemetry/npu has ever arrived; the only "
                             f"evidence is an npu_fault: {store.npu_fault_active}")
        return S_UNKNOWN, ("no telemetry/npu has ever arrived - fpms-npud may "
                           "not be running. This is NOT evidence that detection "
                           "works, and it is not evidence that it is broken.")

    if age > th.npu_stale_s:
        # fpms-npud publishes telemetry UNCONDITIONALLY, including while
        # faulted, precisely so silence means the daemon is gone rather than
        # merely unhappy. Silence is therefore its own diagnosis.
        extra = f"; last event {store.npu_fault_active}" if store.npu_fault_active else ""
        return S_STALE, (f"telemetry/npu silent for {age:.1f}s (bound "
                         f"{th.npu_stale_s:.1f}s); fpms-npud publishes even "
                         f"while faulted, so silence means the daemon is "
                         f"gone{extra}")

    p = store.npu or {}
    det = p.get("detection_available")
    if det is None:
        return S_UNKNOWN, ("telemetry/npu is arriving but carries no "
                           "detection_available field; refusing to guess")
    if det is False:
        fault = p.get("fault") or "unnamed"
        return S_FAULT, (f"fpms-npud reports detection_available=false "
                         f"(fault: {fault}) - THE ROVER IS NOT DETECTING")
    if p.get("model_sha_state") == "mismatch":
        return S_DEGRADED, "the loaded model contradicts /etc/fpms/models.json"
    if p.get("core_mask_fallback"):
        return S_DEGRADED, (f"detecting, but on core mask {p.get('core_mask')} "
                            f"after a fallback from "
                            f"{p.get('core_mask_requested')}")
    return S_OK, "detecting"


# ============================================================================
# SNAPSHOTS
# ============================================================================

def camera_snapshot(store, now, seq=0):
    th = store.th
    state, why = camera_state(store, now)
    age = store.camera_age(now)
    p_age = store.pose_age(now)
    cam = store.cam or {}

    fresh = age is not None and age <= th.camera_stale_s
    # Gated on the POSE feed, not the camera feed. agent_fps is derived from the
    # heartbeat's monotonic `frames` counter, so its freshness is the
    # heartbeat's; gating it on camera freshness would delete it at exactly the
    # moment it is most valuable - frames stopping HERE while the agent's own
    # counter keeps climbing is the signature of a broker/WiFi loss rather than
    # a dead camera, and those two must not look alike.
    pose_fresh = p_age is not None and p_age <= th.pose_stale_s
    if age is None:
        fps = None                       # never seen: NOT zero
    elif fresh:
        fps = round(store.cam_rate.hz, 2)
    else:
        fps = 0.0                        # seen, now stale: zero is the truth

    return {
        "component": "camera",
        "state": state,
        "why": why,
        "stale": (None if age is None else not fresh),
        "frame_age_s": (None if age is None else round(age, 2)),
        "fps": fps,
        "fps_nominal": th.camera_fps_nominal,
        "agent_fps": store.agent_fps if pose_fresh else None,
        "resolution": cam.get("resolution") if fresh else None,
        "width": cam.get("width") if fresh else None,
        "height": cam.get("height") if fresh else None,
        "detections": cam.get("detections") if fresh else None,
        "fire_like": cam.get("fire_like") if fresh else None,
        "agent_npu": cam.get("agent_npu") if fresh else None,
        "frames_received": store.cam_n,
        # Nulled the moment the heartbeat goes stale. `camera_ok` from a
        # heartbeat that stopped a minute ago is the agent's last OPINION, not
        # an observation, and republishing it every second would render a dead
        # agent's parting words as the current state of the camera.
        "agent_frames": (store.pose or {}).get("frames") if pose_fresh else None,
        "agent_camera_ok": (store.pose or {}).get("camera_ok") if pose_fresh else None,
        "agent_streaming": (store.pose or {}).get("streaming") if pose_fresh else None,
        "agent_heartbeat": ("never" if p_age is None else
                            ("ok" if p_age <= th.pose_stale_s else "stale")),
        "agent_heartbeat_age_s": (None if p_age is None else round(p_age, 2)),
        "last_camera_fault": store.cam_fault_last,
        "camera_fault_active": store.cam_fault,
        "warn_s": th.camera_warn_s,
        "stale_s": th.camera_stale_s,
        "mqtt": store.mqtt,
        "bridge_uptime_s": round(now - store.started, 1),
        "seq": seq,
        # Wall clock, for a human reading a log. NEVER for freshness - see the
        # stale contract in the module docstring.
        "ts": round(time.time(), 3),
    }


def npu_snapshot(store, now, seq=0):
    th = store.th
    state, why = npu_state(store, now)
    age = store.npu_age(now)
    fresh = age is not None and age <= th.npu_stale_s
    p = store.npu if fresh else {}
    p = p or {}

    out = {
        "component": "npu",
        "state": state,
        "why": why,
        "stale": (None if age is None else not fresh),
        "telemetry_age_s": (None if age is None else round(age, 2)),
        "telemetry_received": store.npu_n,
        "fault_active": store.npu_fault_active,
        "last_event": store.npu_event,
        "stale_s": th.npu_stale_s,
        "mqtt": store.mqtt,
        "bridge_uptime_s": round(now - store.started, 1),
        "seq": seq,
        "ts": round(time.time(), 3),
    }
    # Every mirrored field is present ONLY while the source is fresh. A stale
    # p90 is not a slow NPU, it is no NPU, and the two must not look alike.
    for k in ("detection_available", "required", "model_loaded",
              "model_sha_state", "model_sha_note", "model_path", "variant",
              "core_mask", "core_mask_requested", "core_mask_fallback",
              "p50_ms", "p90_ms", "max_ms", "probe_ms", "budget_ms",
              "infer_per_s", "inferences", "latency_samples", "drops",
              "timeouts", "errors", "slow", "consecutive_failures",
              "consecutive_slow", "reinits", "queued", "clients", "uptime_s",
              "fault", "fault_detail", "fault_since"):
        out[k] = p.get(k) if fresh else None
    return out


# ============================================================================
# PUBLISH PLAN
# ============================================================================
# A list of (topic, kind, value). Built as data so the ENTIRE publish decision -
# including every "omit this because the source is silent" - is testable with no
# ROS, no MQTT and no clock. The node then executes the plan and does no
# thinking of its own.

def plan_camera(snap):
    plan = [(T_CAM_STATE, "str", snap["state"]),
            (T_CAM_HEALTH, "str", to_json(snap))]
    # ONCE SEEN, then forever: these two are the topics ABOUT staleness, so they
    # must keep talking while stale. Before the first frame there is no age to
    # report and `stale=false` would be a lie, so they are absent instead.
    if snap["frame_age_s"] is not None:
        plan.append((T_CAM_AGE, "f32", snap["frame_age_s"]))
    if snap["stale"] is not None:
        plan.append((T_CAM_STALE, "bool", snap["stale"]))
    # WHILE FRESH only.
    if snap["fps"] is not None and snap["stale"] is False:
        plan.append((T_CAM_FPS, "f32", snap["fps"]))
    if snap["resolution"]:
        plan.append((T_CAM_RES, "str", snap["resolution"]))
    return plan


def plan_npu(snap):
    plan = [(T_NPU_STATE, "str", snap["state"]),
            (T_NPU_HEALTH, "str", to_json(snap))]
    for topic, kind, key in (
            (T_NPU_DET, "bool", "detection_available"),
            (T_NPU_LOADED, "bool", "model_loaded"),
            (T_NPU_SHA, "str", "model_sha_state"),
            (T_NPU_P50, "f32", "p50_ms"),
            (T_NPU_P90, "f32", "p90_ms"),
            (T_NPU_RATE, "f32", "infer_per_s"),
            (T_NPU_DROPS, "i32", "drops"),
            (T_NPU_CONSEC, "i32", "consecutive_failures"),
            (T_NPU_CORES, "str", "core_mask")):
        v = snap.get(key)
        if v is None:
            # UNKNOWN. Not 0, not false, not "ok". The topic simply does not
            # tick, and every consumer of this stack already treats a silent
            # topic correctly.
            continue
        plan.append((topic, kind, v))
    return plan


def plan_all(cam_snap, npu_snap):
    return plan_camera(cam_snap) + plan_npu(npu_snap)


# ============================================================================
# MQTT INGESTION - the exact entry point paho's on_message calls
# ============================================================================

def mqtt_suffix(topic, thing):
    """"fpms/rover1/telemetry/npu" -> "telemetry/npu", or None if foreign."""
    prefix = f"fpms/{thing}/"
    if not topic.startswith(prefix):
        return None
    return topic[len(prefix):]


TELEMETRY_SUFFIXES = ("telemetry/camera", "telemetry/pose", "telemetry/npu")
EVENT_SUFFIXES = ("events/npu_fault", "events/npu_recovered",
                  "events/fault", "events/camera_recovered")
ALL_SUFFIXES = TELEMETRY_SUFFIXES + EVENT_SUFFIXES


def handle_mqtt_message(store, topic, raw, now, thing):
    """Ingest one MQTT message. Returns (suffix, mirror_dict_or_None).

    Called from paho's network thread. MUST NOT RAISE - an exception here kills
    the network loop and the whole node goes silent, which is the failure mode
    this file exists to eliminate rather than reproduce.
    """
    try:
        suffix = mqtt_suffix(topic, thing)
        if suffix is None or suffix not in ALL_SUFFIXES:
            store.unknown_topics += 1
            return None, None
        try:
            payload = json.loads(raw.decode("utf-8") if isinstance(raw, (bytes, bytearray))
                                 else raw)
        except (ValueError, UnicodeDecodeError, AttributeError):
            store.bad_payloads += 1
            return suffix, None
        if not isinstance(payload, dict):
            store.bad_payloads += 1
            return suffix, None

        if suffix == "telemetry/camera":
            store.note_camera(payload, now)
            return suffix, None
        if suffix == "telemetry/pose":
            store.note_pose(payload, now)
            return suffix, None
        if suffix == "telemetry/npu":
            store.note_npu(payload, now)
            return suffix, None
        return suffix, store.note_event(suffix, payload, now)
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001 - see the docstring; never raise
        store.bad_payloads += 1
        log(f"mqtt handler error on {topic}: {e}")
        return None, None


# ============================================================================
# NODE
# ============================================================================

class TelemetryBridge(Node):
    """MQTT camera/NPU health -> ROS 2 std_msgs under /fpms/."""

    def __init__(self, cfg):
        super().__init__("fpms_telemetry_ros")
        self.thing = cfg.get("FPMS_THING_NAME", "rover1")
        self.th = Thresholds(cfg)
        self.store = Store(self.th, started=time.monotonic())
        self._lock = threading.Lock()
        self._seq = 0
        self._published = 0
        self._plan_errors = 0
        self._seen_first = set()

        # RELIABLE + VOLATILE, depth 10 - byte for byte the QoS that
        # fpms_foxglove_cmd.py's /fpms/mission/* mirror uses and that this
        # dashboard's rosbridge subscriptions already consume successfully.
        # Not TRANSIENT_LOCAL: a latched last value would let a late subscriber
        # read a value from before the outage as if it were current, which is
        # exactly the lie this node is built to prevent. At 1 Hz a new
        # subscriber waits at most one second for the truth instead.
        qos = QoSProfile(reliability=ReliabilityPolicy.RELIABLE,
                         durability=DurabilityPolicy.VOLATILE,
                         history=HistoryPolicy.KEEP_LAST, depth=10)
        types = {"str": String, "bool": Bool, "f32": Float32, "i32": Int32}
        self.pubs = {}
        for topic, kind in TOPIC_KINDS.items():
            self.pubs[topic] = (self.create_publisher(types[kind], topic, qos),
                                types[kind], kind)

        self._assert_publishers()

        self.topics_in = [f"fpms/{self.thing}/{s}" for s in ALL_SUFFIXES]
        self.store.mqtt["expected"] = len(self.topics_in)

        period = 1.0 / self.th.publish_hz
        self.create_timer(period, self._tick)
        self.create_timer(max(5.0, self.th.report_s), self._report)

        log(f"thing={self.thing} publishing {len(self.pubs)} topics under "
            f"{TOPIC_PREFIX} at {self.th.publish_hz:.2f} Hz")
        log(f"bounds: camera warn={self.th.camera_warn_s:.1f}s "
            f"stale={self.th.camera_stale_s:.1f}s | pose stale="
            f"{self.th.pose_stale_s:.1f}s | npu stale={self.th.npu_stale_s:.1f}s")
        log("publishers on actuation topics: 0 (this node cannot move the rover)")

    # -- guardrail ----------------------------------------------------------

    def _assert_publishers(self):
        """Verify the OUTCOME - rclpy's own publisher list - not our intent.

        Two properties, both enforced by aborting the process rather than by a
        comment, because a comment decays:

          1. Nothing outside /fpms/. A topic outside the prefix would be
             published perfectly happily by ROS and then dropped by rosbridge's
             topics_glob without a word - connected, subscribed, silent, which
             is this project's signature failure.
          2. Nothing that can move the rover. /cmd_vel and /cmd_duty are absent
             from that whitelist BY CONSTRUCTION and must be absent from here by
             construction too.
        """
        actual = sorted(p.topic_name for p in self.publishers)
        bad_prefix = [t for t in actual if not t.startswith(TOPIC_PREFIX)]
        forbidden = [t for t in actual if t in FORBIDDEN_TOPICS]
        missing = sorted(set(TOPIC_KINDS) - set(actual))
        if forbidden:
            raise SystemExit(
                f"REFUSING TO START: fpms-telemetry-ros created publishers on "
                f"{forbidden}. This node is a READ-OUT. Whatever added that "
                f"publisher belongs in fpms_teleop or fpms_missions.")
        if bad_prefix:
            raise SystemExit(
                f"REFUSING TO START: {bad_prefix} is outside {TOPIC_PREFIX} and "
                f"is therefore outside topics_glob in "
                f"/etc/fpms/rosbridge_params.yaml. It would publish and never "
                f"reach the dashboard, with no error anywhere. Rename it under "
                f"{TOPIC_PREFIX}; do NOT widen the whitelist.")
        if missing:
            raise SystemExit(
                f"REFUSING TO START: create_publisher did not produce {missing}. "
                f"The publisher table and the ROS graph disagree.")

    # -- MQTT side ----------------------------------------------------------

    def on_mqtt_message(self, topic, payload):
        """paho network thread. Fast, and never raises."""
        now = time.monotonic()
        with self._lock:
            suffix, mirror = handle_mqtt_message(self.store, topic, payload,
                                                 now, self.thing)
        if suffix and suffix not in self._seen_first:
            self._seen_first.add(suffix)
            log(f"first {suffix} received")
        if mirror is None:
            return
        # Mirrored immediately rather than on the next tick: an event is an
        # interruption and a second of latency on a fault is a second the
        # operator is not looking at it.
        topic_out = T_NPU_FAULT if suffix.startswith("events/npu") else T_AGENT_FAULT
        try:
            self._publish_one(topic_out, "str", to_json(mirror, MAX_EVENT_CHARS))
            log(f"mirrored {suffix} -> {topic_out}: "
                f"{mirror.get('fault') or mirror.get('error') or mirror.get('cleared') or ''}")
        except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001
            log(f"event mirror failed for {suffix}: {e}")

    def on_mqtt_state(self, state, detail=""):
        with self._lock:
            self.store.mqtt["state"] = state
            self.store.mqtt["since"] = round(time.time(), 1)
            if detail:
                self.store.mqtt["detail"] = detail

    def on_mqtt_suback(self, topic, granted_ok, code):
        """SUBACK, not the return of subscribe(). See the module docstring."""
        with self._lock:
            if granted_ok:
                self.store.mqtt["subscribed"] += 1
            else:
                self.store.mqtt["refused"].append({"topic": topic,
                                                   "code": str(code)})
        if not granted_ok:
            log(f"*** SUBSCRIBE REFUSED for {topic} (code {code}). The broker "
                f"ACL does not grant it. This looks EXACTLY like a dead sensor "
                f"from the dashboard - check /etc/mosquitto/fpms.acl against "
                f"FPMS_THING_NAME={self.thing}. ***")

    # -- publish ------------------------------------------------------------

    def _publish_one(self, topic, kind, value):
        entry = self.pubs.get(topic)
        if entry is None:
            # A plan naming a topic with no publisher. Impossible today because
            # _assert_publishers() checked the table, but a value that quietly
            # goes nowhere is the exact class of bug this stack keeps paying
            # for, so it is counted and named rather than dropped.
            self._plan_errors += 1
            log(f"plan names {topic} but there is no publisher for it")
            return False
        pub, msgcls, want = entry
        if want != kind:
            self._plan_errors += 1
            log(f"plan gave a {kind} for {topic}, which is {want}")
            return False
        msg = msgcls()
        if kind == "str":
            msg.data = value if isinstance(value, str) else to_json(value)
        elif kind == "bool":
            msg.data = bool(value)
        elif kind == "f32":
            f = _num(value)
            if f is None:
                return False
            msg.data = float(f)
        else:
            i = _int(value)
            if i is None:
                return False
            msg.data = int(i)
        pub.publish(msg)
        self._published += 1
        return True

    def _tick(self):
        now = time.monotonic()
        with self._lock:
            self._seq += 1
            seq = self._seq
            cam = camera_snapshot(self.store, now, seq)
            npu = npu_snapshot(self.store, now, seq)
        for topic, kind, value in plan_all(cam, npu):
            try:
                self._publish_one(topic, kind, value)
            except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001
                self._plan_errors += 1
                log(f"publish failed on {topic}: {e}")

    def _report(self):
        now = time.monotonic()
        with self._lock:
            s = self.store
            cam = camera_state(s, now)[0]
            npu = npu_state(s, now)[0]
            counts = (s.cam_n, s.pose_n, s.npu_n, s.bad_payloads)
        log(f"camera={cam} npu={npu} | rx camera={counts[0]} pose={counts[1]} "
            f"npu={counts[2]} bad={counts[3]} | published={self._published} "
            f"plan_errors={self._plan_errors} mqtt={self.store.mqtt['state']}")


# ============================================================================
# MAIN
# ============================================================================

def main():
    if not _ROS_AVAILABLE:
        log("rclpy/std_msgs not importable - source /opt/ros/humble/setup.bash")
        return 1
    try:
        import paho.mqtt.client as mqtt
        # VERSION2 unguarded, on purpose: the image pins paho >= 2.0 precisely
        # because five files import this symbol. A guarded fallback here would
        # let a downgraded paho run this node with v1 callback signatures, whose
        # arguments differ, and the failure would be a handler that never fires.
        from paho.mqtt.client import CallbackAPIVersion
    except ImportError as e:
        log(f"paho.mqtt is not importable ({e}); this node cannot run")
        return 1

    cfg = load_config()
    rclpy.init()
    node = TelemetryBridge(cfg)

    # mid -> topic, so a SUBACK can be attributed to the subscription that
    # caused it. paho gives the mid at subscribe time and only the mid back.
    pending = {}
    pending_lock = threading.Lock()

    def on_connect(client, userdata, flags, rc, properties=None):
        node.on_mqtt_state("connected", str(rc))
        log(f"MQTT connected to {cfg.get('FPMS_MQTT_HOST')}:"
            f"{cfg.get('FPMS_MQTT_PORT')} ({rc})")
        for topic in node.topics_in:
            # QoS 0 for telemetry, 1 for events. Telemetry is a description of
            # NOW and a replayed queue of it after a reconnect is a queue of
            # lies; an event is an interruption that must not be lost. Same
            # split fpms-npud publishes with.
            qos = 1 if "/events/" in topic else 0
            try:
                rc_sub, mid = client.subscribe(topic, qos=qos)
            except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001
                log(f"subscribe({topic}) raised: {e}")
                node.on_mqtt_suback(topic, False, f"raised: {e}")
                continue
            if rc_sub != mqtt.MQTT_ERR_SUCCESS:
                node.on_mqtt_suback(topic, False, f"queue rc={rc_sub}")
                continue
            with pending_lock:
                pending[mid] = topic
        # Subscriptions are NOT counted here. The count that matters is the one
        # the broker granted, and that arrives in on_subscribe below.

    def on_subscribe(client, userdata, mid, reason_codes, properties=None):
        with pending_lock:
            topic = pending.pop(mid, f"<mid {mid}>")
        codes = reason_codes if isinstance(reason_codes, (list, tuple)) else [reason_codes]
        for code in codes:
            failed = getattr(code, "is_failure", None)
            if failed is None:
                # v3.1.1 granted-QoS ints: 0x80 is the refusal.
                try:
                    failed = int(code) >= 128
                except (TypeError, ValueError):
                    failed = False
            node.on_mqtt_suback(topic, not failed, code)

    def on_message(client, userdata, msg):
        node.on_mqtt_message(msg.topic, msg.payload)

    def on_disconnect(client, userdata, flags, rc, properties=None):
        node.on_mqtt_state("disconnected", str(rc))
        log(f"MQTT disconnected ({rc}); paho will retry")

    thing = cfg.get("FPMS_THING_NAME", "rover1")
    client = mqtt.Client(client_id=f"{thing}-telemetry-ros",
                         callback_api_version=CallbackAPIVersion.VERSION2)
    user = cfg.get("FPMS_MQTT_USER")
    if user:
        client.username_pw_set(user, cfg.get("FPMS_MQTT_PASS", ""))
    client.on_connect = on_connect
    client.on_subscribe = on_subscribe
    client.on_message = on_message
    client.on_disconnect = on_disconnect

    host = cfg.get("FPMS_MQTT_HOST", "127.0.0.1")
    port = int(cfg.get("FPMS_MQTT_PORT", "1883") or 1883)
    try:
        client.connect(host, port, keepalive=30)
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001
        # Do not die. systemd would restart us into the same broker outage, and
        # a restarting node is one that stops publishing "unknown" - which is
        # the only honest thing to say during an outage.
        log(f"initial MQTT connect to {host}:{port} failed ({e}); will retry")
        node.on_mqtt_state("connecting", str(e))
    client.loop_start()

    stopping = threading.Event()

    def stop(*_):
        stopping.set()

    signal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, stop)
    signal.signal(signal.SIGINT, stop)

    try:
        while not stopping.is_set():
            rclpy.spin_once(node, timeout_sec=0.2)
    finally:
        client.loop_stop()
        try:
            client.disconnect()
        except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001
            pass
        node.destroy_node()
        rclpy.shutdown()
        log("stopped")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
